sec('2. Part 2 - lecture answers')
h('Q1. Dependency confusion')
p('In merged-index mode, a resolver considers compatible candidates from both sources; it does not treat the private index as authoritative. In the simulation, public 99.0.0 wins over private 1.4.0. A higher compatible public version can therefore substitute an internal dependency. Important correction to the simplified question: an exact requirement ==1.4.0 excludes 99.0.0; the example concerns two available versions that the requirement permits, not bypass of an exact pin. A pin alone still does not authenticate the bytes or source of a same-version artifact. [R1]')
h('Q2. Typosquatting and install-time execution')
p('A misspelled name such as reqeusts can select an unrelated attacker-controlled distribution. Source-distribution build backends or legacy setup.py steps can execute during build/install before application imports, so avoiding an import does not eliminate that risk. This is a possibility, not a claim that every wheel executes setup.py: standard wheel installation has a different mechanism. Use vetted names and sources, reviewed hashes and isolated builds; never install the illustrative look-alikes. [R1, R4]')
h('Q3. SBOM, CycloneDX and SPDX')
p('An SBOM is a structured inventory of components, versions, identifiers and relationships. CycloneDX and SPDX provide interoperable formats for that information. It lets responders identify potentially affected artifacts when a dependency advisory appears; this report binds the inventory to the image digest. The worksheet calls it a prerequisite for provenance: it is useful supporting inventory, but an SBOM is not itself build provenance and is not a universal formal prerequisite for a SLSA level. Provenance must describe the build and its inputs. [R5, R6, R8]')
h('Q4. Keyless signing: Sigstore, Fulcio, Rekor and OIDC')
p('OIDC authenticates the signer; Fulcio issues a short-lived certificate binding an ephemeral key to that identity. Rekor records public signing evidence for auditability. This reduces exposure from a reusable long-lived private key, including hard-coded-key risk (CWE-321), but does not remove account compromise or malicious-code risk. Our approved GitHub signing disclosed the confirmed email, and exact-policy verification required that email plus the GitHub OAuth issuer. Registry authentication and OIDC authentication are separate. [R9, R10]')
h('Q5. SLSA levels and the evidence-based assessment')
p('The approved SLSA v1.2 Build track has L0 (no guarantees), L1 (build provenance exists), L2 (authenticated provenance generated by a hosted build platform), and L3 (a hardened build platform resisting tampering). The historical unnamed 1-4 model is not the current Build-track model. A signature, SBOM and a gate do not automatically establish a level: provenance contents and platform guarantees must be assessed. We claim no SLSA level, because the verified statement is an artifact-signature predicate and no complete trusted-builder provenance policy was evaluated. [R7, R8]')
