code('ERROR: Hashes are required in --require-hashes mode, but they are missing from some requirements.\nFlask==1.1.4 --hash=sha256:c34f04500f2cbbea882b1acb02002ad6fe6b7ffa64a6164577995657f50aed22')
p('This is a genuine refusal after downloading the Flask wheel to compute its hash. It does not produce a fully hashed lockfile or demonstrate an actual malicious replacement. An approved lock must cover all resolved dependencies and permitted artifacts using reviewed hashes; copying a hash from an untrusted download alone does not establish trust.')
p('PyPI was the single source for this public-dependency demonstration, not a claimed private internal registry. A real team should use one controlled private/curated proxy containing internal artifacts and vetted public dependencies. Merging sources with --extra-index-url permits competing candidates; a single index works only when alternate config, environment, find-links and direct references are controlled. The existing Task 2 images show the same pair changing from public 99.0.0 to private 1.4.0. [R1]')
h('7.2 Namespace scoping and the strongest defense')
p('Practical proposal: define an organization-specific internal naming convention, enforce allowed package names and access controls in a private registry, and use one curated index. Reserving a public name where policy permits can reduce collisions, but a Python prefix is not enforceable namespace ownership or routing. No name was reserved or package published in this exercise.')
p('Student-confirmed choice: single curated trusted index. Suggested rationale - review before adopting: it removes the competing public source at the point of candidate selection and centralizes policy, directly matching the simulation before/after result. It is not sufficient alone: registry compromise and maintainer compromise remain, so reviewed hash locks, SCA and the signed-digest gate are complementary.')
p('Remediation note (A03/A08; CWE-1357/CWE-829): enforce one vetted source and reviewed hash locks to reduce substitution and unapproved bytes. Keep SCA and compatibility testing because integrity controls do not fix the known CVEs (CWE-1395).')
