sec('8. Part 4 - security analysis and reflection')
h('8.1 Required finding/tool/mapping/fix table')
table(['Finding','Tool / evidence','OWASP / CWE','Fix / limitation'],[['Known vulnerable packages','sca_scan.sh, saved Trivy/pip-audit','A03 / CWE-1395; CWE-1104 only if unmaintained','Compatible patched dependency set; proposed, not implemented'],['Confused source selection','Resolver simulation','A03 / CWE-1357, CWE-829','Single curated index plus naming/access policy'],['Unhashed requirement','pip --require-hashes refusal','A03/A08 / CWE-1357, CWE-829','Complete reviewed hash lock; refusal only demonstrated'],['Unsigned or unexpected signer','Cosign positive/negative and gate','A08 / CWE-829','Enforce digest, exact identity/issuer and failure handling'],['Reusable signing-key exposure','Keyless Fulcio/Rekor flow','A08 / CWE-321 risk reduction','Ephemeral signing key; protect the identity account']])
h('8.2 XZ Utils backdoor - CVE-2024-3094')
p('Andres Freund reported the upstream xz/liblzma backdoor on 29 March 2024 after investigating abnormal SSH behavior. The affected release tarballs included 5.6.0 and 5.6.1; part of the injected build logic was in release tarballs rather than the corresponding upstream file, illustrating why inspecting only a repository snapshot can miss release-time manipulation. The malicious build path conditionally modified liblzma output. This is analyzed here as A03 supplier/build compromise and A08 artifact-integrity failure, with CWE-829/CWE-1357 as reasoned classroom mappings, not a claim that these are the CVE record\'s official assigned CWE. [R11, R2, R4]')
p('An SBOM can help find affected xz versions but does not identify malicious intent by itself. A valid upstream signature may faithfully authenticate a malicious release made through a compromised trusted maintainer. A provenance gate helps only when it verifies meaningful trusted source/build expectations and input relationships; matching an approved signer alone would not necessarily catch this backdoor. Independent release review, comparison of generated tarballs to source, controlled builds and behavioral investigation provide additional evidence. These are analysis and recommendations, not tests performed against XZ in this lab. [R11, R8]')
h('8.3 Suggested mitigation and SLSA self-assessment - student review required')
p('Suggested reflection, not a statement of private experience: The evidence demonstrates a component inventory, an identity-bound signature, unsigned-image rejection, and a signature-gated simulated deployment. It does not establish a SLSA level because the BuildKit attestation was not fully assessed and no trusted build-platform provenance policy was verified. The student chose a single curated trusted index as the strongest defense; the proposed reason is that it removes competing public candidates before installation, while hashes and verified release digests address different integrity boundaries. Review and rewrite this paragraph in your own words before submitting. [R7, R8]')
h('8.4 Evidence & Integrity: suggested own-words explanations')
p('Suggested answer to “What did you do and why did the vulnerability work?”: The controlled resolver simulation pooled private and public candidates and selected a higher public version. The SCA scans separately showed known advisories in the intentionally old dependency tree; they did not demonstrate that every endpoint was exploitable.')
p('Suggested answer to “Why does the fix stop it and what could still break it?”: One curated source excludes the competing index, hashes constrain acceptable bytes, and exact-identity verification requires approval of the same immutable digest. Compromised trusted sources/accounts, unreviewed hashes, gate bypass or malicious-but-authorized dependencies can still defeat those assumptions. These explanations are AI-assisted suggestions awaiting student adoption.')
